Campus Reachability and Emergency Route Audit
Using Recursive DFS and BFS in Python
Submitted By
Name: Kanuri Thanush

Roll Number: 25MVCAIR0059

Course: MCA

Language Used: Python 3
Problem Statement
The objective of this project is to develop a graph-based campus auditing system that analyzes the connectivity of different areas within a campus. The campus consists of several locations represented as vertices, while the walkways connecting these locations are represented as undirected edges.

The application performs three major operations:
1. Identify all connected components using Recursive Depth First Search (DFS).
2. Perform DFS and BFS traversals from a given emergency station (source vertex).
3. Find the shortest route from the emergency station to requested destination vertices using the Breadth First Search parent array.
The program must always process neighbouring vertices in increasing numerical order so that every execution produces the same output regardless of the order in which edges are entered.
Objective
The main objectives of this project are:
- To represent a campus as an undirected graph.
- To identify every connected component of the graph.
- To assign every vertex the smallest numbered vertex of its connected component.
- To perform Recursive DFS traversal.
- To perform Queue-based BFS traversal.
- To compute minimum-edge routes using BFS.
- To print unreachable destinations when no path exists.
- To produce deterministic outputs by sorting adjacency lists.
Introduction
Graphs are one of the most important data structures used in computer science. Many real-world problems such as road navigation, computer networks, social networks, transportation systems, and campus maps can be represented using graphs.

In this project, every building or location inside the campus is represented as a vertex, while every walkway connecting two buildings is represented as an edge.

Example:
Academic Block ----- Library
        |
        |
Computer Lab ----- Cafeteria

Hostel ----- Playground

This graph contains two connected areas.

The first connected area contains:
Academic Block
Library
Computer Lab
Cafeteria

The second connected area contains:
Hostel
Playground

The program automatically detects these connected areas and labels them.
Concepts Used
The project uses the following graph concepts:
1. Graph
A graph consists of vertices and edges.

Example
