Time Complexity Analysis
The efficiency of the program depends on the graph traversal algorithms used. Both Depth First Search (DFS) and Breadth First Search (BFS) are efficient graph traversal techniques.
1. Building the Graph
The program reads all the edges and stores them in an adjacency list.
adj[u].append(v)adj[v].append(u)


There are m edges.

Time Complexity
\[
O(m)
\]
where
- n = Number of vertices
- m = Number of edges
2. Sorting the Adjacency List
Every adjacency list is sorted.
for lst in adj:    lst.sort()


Sorting guarantees neighbours are visited in increasing order.

Worst-case complexity
\[
O(m\log n)
\]
3. Connected Component DFS
Every vertex is visited exactly once.

Every edge is also visited once.

Time Complexity
\[
O(n+m)
\]
4. DFS Traversal
The DFS traversal starts only from the source vertex.

Every reachable vertex is visited once.

Time Complexity
\[
O(n+m)
\]
5. BFS Traversal
Breadth First Search also visits every reachable vertex only once.

Each edge is examined only once.

Time Complexity
\[
O(n+m)
\]
6. Route Reconstruction
For every query, the program follows the parent array.

If the route contains k vertices

Time Complexity
\[
O(k)
\]
Worst case
\[
O(n)
\]
Overall Time Complexity
The complete program executes in
\[
O(m\log n)+O(n+m)
\]
which is commonly written as
\[
\boxed{O(m\log n+n+m)}
\]
Since the maximum number of vertices is only 200, the program executes very quickly.
Space Complexity
The program stores several arrays.
Data Structure	Space
Adjacency List	O(n+m)
Visited Array	O(n)
Label Array	O(n)
Parent Array	O(n)
Distance Array	O(n)
Queue	O(n)
DFS Order	O(n)
BFS Order	O(n)


Total Space Complexity
\[
\boxed{O(n+m)}
\]
