Conclusion
This project successfully demonstrates the implementation of graph traversal algorithms using Recursive Depth First Search (DFS) and Breadth First Search (BFS). The program identifies all connected components in the graph, labels each vertex with the smallest vertex number in its connected component, performs deterministic DFS and BFS traversals, and reconstructs minimum-edge routes using the BFS parent array.

By sorting adjacency lists before traversal, the output remains consistent and reproducible regardless of the order in which edges are provided. The project also highlights the practical use of graphs in representing campus walkways and emergency navigation systems. Overall, the implementation satisfies all the requirements specified in the problem statement while maintaining efficient time and space complexity

